{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "ShokoohSaremi2004-Cband-cube-PBS-GA",
  "Source": "M. Shokooh-Saremi, M. Nourian, M. M. Mirsalehi, S. H. Keshmiri, \"Design of multilayer polarizing beam splitters using genetic algorithm,\" Optics Communications 233, 57-65 (2004). doi:10.1016/j.optcom.2004.01.008 - Table 2, column GA (p. 63); design parameters in text p. 62",
  "Notes": "C-band cube polarizing beam splitter (reflect S, transmit P) for WDM, designed by genetic algorithm\nSpecification: C-band (1528-1560 nm) cube PBS, 45 deg in prism (n = 1.5 BK7 glass), max 20 layers, SiO2 (1.465) and Ta2O5 (2.065); maximize fitness F = 1/sum(|Rs-1|+|Rp|) over 10 wavelength points (Rs -> 1, Rp -> 0).\nReported: 19 layers; sum d = 4.7372 um; sum(nd) = 8.2401 um; degree of polarization P = |Rs-Rp|/(Rs+Rp) over 1528-1560 nm: max 1.0, min 0.9954, mean 0.9986, std 0.0012; fitness F = 28.65 (10 wavelengths); yield 100 % under +/-5 A random errors. Fig. 8: Rs ~99.9 % at 1520 nm falling to ~99.5 % at 1570 nm; Fig. 9: Rp < ~0.75 % over 1520-1570 nm.\nNotes: Table 2 lists A (incident, n=1.5) first, then layers, then S (substrate, n=1.5), so the table order is already incident-to-substrate. Thicknesses given in Angstrom in the paper, converted to nm (/10). Materials treated as lossless, dispersionless (paper: 'materials are considered to be perfect dielectrics'). Angle 45 deg is in the n=1.5 incident medium. Wavelength sampling: 10 points over the band (exact points not given; step 3.556 nm assumes evenly spaced including endpoints). Recomputed sum d and sum nd match the table exactly.\nLayers from Table 2, column GA (p. 63); design parameters in text p. 62; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours total 4737.2 nm; P min 0.9954, mean 0.9984; paper total 4737.2 nm; P min 0.9954, mean 0.9986.",
  "Wavelength": [
    1528,
    1531.556,
